---

<ContentLayout
  title="CRO Website Audit: Measurement and Experiment Planning"
  description="Plan a conversion rate optimisation audit with defined events, comparable segments, verified customer journeys and a testable experiment queue."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Website Audit', href: '/website-audit/' },
    { label: 'CRO Audit', href: '/cro-website-audit/' },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  publishDate="2026-03-28"
  updateDate="2026-09-11"
>
<p>A CRO audit reviews conversion measurement and customer journeys to produce a prioritised experiment plan. Define the desired action and denominator before comparing rates. A visit, a completed form and a qualified enquiry are different outcomes.</p>
<h2 id="what-is">What does a CRO audit establish?</h2>
<p>The audit establishes the measured funnel, confirmed defects and hypotheses worth testing. It does not guarantee a revenue increase or reveal the cause of every abandoned session.</p>
<p>Illustrative arithmetic: 200 purchases from 10,000 eligible sessions is a 2% purchase rate. At the same session count, 250 purchases would be 2.5%: a rise of 0.5 percentage points or 25% relative. That is not an AuditWeb result or forecast; revenue also depends on order value, refunds and other factors.</p>
<h2 id="vs-ux">How does CRO differ from a UX audit?</h2>
<p>CRO focuses on a defined conversion outcome and experiment decisions. A <a href="/website-ux-audit/">UX audit</a> considers the broader experience, including comprehension, task completion and accessibility. Use both when improving a metric could otherwise make the experience worse.</p>
<p>For a detailed inspection of landing pages, forms and checkout, follow the <a href="/website-conversion-audit/">conversion journey audit</a>.</p>
<h2 id="analytics">Which analytics checks come first?</h2>
<p>Confirm that the event represents a real action before analysing the funnel. A button click is not proof of a successful enquiry, and a publicly accessible confirmation page can overcount completion.</p>
<ol><li>Define the event, eligible population, date range and reporting timezone.</li><li>Reproduce the action with an agreed test account and compare the browser event with its destination.</li><li>Inspect duplicate events, consent effects, internal traffic and failed submissions.</li><li>Compare device, landing page and traffic source using consistent definitions.</li><li>Record sample size and uncertainty before proposing an explanation.</li></ol>
<p>GA4 uses <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/12571843">key events</a> to report important actions. Use the <a href="/website-analytics-audit/">analytics audit</a> when collection or reporting is unreliable. A device gap can reflect audience or task mix rather than a broken mobile interface.</p>
<h2 id="landing-pages">What should landing-page analysis record?</h2>
<p>Record the visitor's entry promise, the information needed to decide and the next action offered. Compare the actual page with its ad, email or search-result context.</p>
<ul><li>Does the headline accurately identify the offer and audience?</li><li>Are price, eligibility, delivery and commitment clear where relevant?</li><li>Can the visitor find supporting evidence before deciding?</li><li>Do the CTA text and destination describe the same action?</li><li>Can the page be used with a keyboard, on a narrow screen and at zoom?</li></ul>
<p>Heatmaps and scroll data show recorded interactions, not eye movements or intent. Use them to choose a question for task testing rather than automatically deleting unread sections.</p>
<h2 id="funnel">How should the conversion funnel be reviewed?</h2>
<p>Trace the journey from entry to confirmed completion and recovery from error. Record each required field, step, dependency and interruption.</p>
<p>Inspect unexpected costs, unavailable delivery options, unclear validation, lost input and account requirements. Remove a field only when the business does not need it at that stage. There is no fixed conversion penalty per field.</p>
<p>Test payment and booking journeys in an authorised test environment. Do not create real orders to obtain a favourable audit screenshot.</p>
<h2 id="trust">Which trust signals are appropriate?</h2>
<p>Use verifiable evidence and accurate terms. Check permission for testimonials, the scope of certifications and the validity of displayed ratings. Do not add invented badges, client logos or urgency.</p>
<p>Make pricing, contact details and refund conditions understandable. A truthful explanation of a limit is more useful than a promise the service cannot meet.</p>
<h2 id="testing">How should experiments be designed?</h2>
<p>Define the hypothesis, primary metric, guardrails, assignment method and stopping rule before launch. Sample requirements depend on baseline rate, the effect worth detecting and the chosen analysis method; no fixed duration or conversion count works for every site.</p>
<p>Repair reproducible defects directly when the expected behaviour is clear. Test uncertain variants as coherent changes. If multiple elements change together, attribute the result to the variant as a whole.</p>
<p>Before-and-after comparisons can be useful for monitoring but are exposed to seasonality and traffic-mix changes. For searchable test variants, follow Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/website-testing">website-testing guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 id="checklist">What belongs in the CRO handoff?</h2>
<ul><li>Defined event, denominator, dates and baseline.</li><li>Evidence for each confirmed defect and a separate hypothesis list.</li><li>Proposed change, affected journey, owner and implementation risk.</li><li>Experiment method, primary metric and guardrails such as lead quality or refunds.</li><li>Retest results and a conclusion of improved, unchanged or inconclusive.</li></ul>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">implementation tracker</a> to preserve the decision and verification evidence.</p>
</ContentLayout>
